<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    A password protects an account. A diary is closer to being the person —
    which makes it the single strongest privacy expectation in this app, and
    the one thing here where the correct amount of cleverness is none.
  </p>
  <p>
    So it's sealed even harder than the password vault, it has no
    intelligence pointed at it, and it appears nowhere else in the app.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">The rules it keeps</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Even the titles are encrypted") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Everything but the date. Somebody with the database file cannot read
        your entries and cannot read what you called them, which is nearly as
        revealing.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "No sharing at all") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Not restricted sharing — none. There is no mechanism, which is a
        stronger promise than a setting, because a setting can be changed by
        somebody who isn't you.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Nothing reads it") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        No summarising, no filing, no mention on the dashboard, no model
        anywhere near it. It doesn't feed the assistant, and that's the
        design rather than an oversight.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "A surface worth returning to") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A focused editor, one entry a day, saved as you write, with a
        calendar that makes the habit visible. Missed days are backfillable,
        because life happens.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">On this day</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    The reason to keep one for years rather than months: what you wrote on
    this date one year ago, and five. It's the only feature here that gets
    better purely by the passage of time, and it's the argument for a diary
    that lives somewhere permanent rather than in an app that might be
    discontinued.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">The trade, stated plainly</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Real secrecy means the contents depend on your passphrase, and losing it
    loses them. There's a recovery route you can set up in advance, on
    purpose, with your eyes open — and nothing that quietly weakens the seal
    to make the software more forgiving.
  </p>
</section>
